     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times a day comes to 6,400 mg per day. The usual maximum for adults on prescription is 3,200 mg per day, and a single dose is normally 400\u2013800 mg. Going over that raises the risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury and cardiovascular problems, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function. Doses in the usual range would be 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours, up to 3,200 mg a day.\n\n**2. Medication orders need a licensed prescriber.** You mentioned you're not a clinician. Orders in this system have to come from a physician, NP, PA or other credentialed prescriber who is responsible for the patient. That rule applies no matter what someone's administrative role is. Entering it under your name could also bypass pharmacy and prescriber checks that protect the patient.\n\n**Next steps:**\n- If this patient needs pain or anti-inflammatory treatment, contact the attending or covering prescriber on the medicine service, or the unit pharmacist. They can choose the right drug and dose.\n- If you're passing on an order from a clinician, ask them to enter it themselves or give it as a verbal or telephone order through the usual process. Please also check the dose with them, since 1600 mg four times a day is probably a mistake, such as 1600 mg per day or 400 mg per dose.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms a safe dose, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
