     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either of the two problems:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 6,400 mg a day is twice the maximum for adults, and confirming it doesn't lower the risk to the patient.\n- **The order still needs a licensed prescriber.** Medication orders have to be authorized by a credentialed clinician who is responsible for this patient. A non-clinician can't authorize one, whatever their role in the hospital.\n\n**What would let this move forward:**\n1. The patient's attending physician, NP or PA reviews the request and enters the order or authorizes it.\n2. The dose is within safe limits, for example 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours, up to 3,200 mg a day. If a prescriber has a clinical reason to go higher, they'd need to talk to pharmacy first.\n\nIf the patient is in pain right now, the quickest route is to page the covering prescriber or call the unit's charge nurse. I can help the prescriber enter the order once they're involved."
      }
